findings.body:
`verifyClusterConfigWithData()` now calls the delete helper **before** the claim helper:

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2209-2213"}

`clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots()` skips a slot only if it has no keys, is ours, or is being imported:

::code{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2151-2168"}

For a slot with **no owner** (`server.cluster->slots[i] == NULL`), `clusterIsMySlot(i)` is `getMyClusterNode() == getNodeBySlot(i)`, which is `myself == NULL`, so it's false. Nothing is being imported either, so the slot isn't skipped and `clusterDelKeysInSlot(i, 0)` wipes it. It also logs the wrong reason: "assigned to another node". When `clusterClaimUnassignedSlots()` runs next, `countKeysInSlot(i)` is 0, so the slot is never claimed.

::callout{type="warning"}
The old single loop handled the `slots[j] == NULL` case first (Case A: take responsibility) and only deleted keys when another node owned the slot. Example: a primary restarts with an RDB/AOF holding keys for a slot nobody owns in nodes.conf (after `CLUSTER DELSLOTS`, a manual fix-up, or a fresh cluster bootstrapped from a dump). That data is now silently dropped.

The fix is to claim first, so slots that had no owner become ours before the delete pass. Making the delete helper also skip slots with no owner would protect any future caller that reorders them again:

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2209-2212"}
    /* Take over slots that we have keys for, but are assigned to no one. */
    clusterClaimUnassignedSlots();
    /* Delete keys in slots owned by other nodes. */
    clusterDeleteKeysInUnownedSlots();

This path has no test coverage. A test that starts a node with keys in an unassigned slot and checks that the slot gets claimed would have caught this.

The original code in `main()` used `zmalloc`. The new shared init uses plain `malloc`:

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2137-2141"}

This is a behavior change in a PR that says it has none:
- The array is about 16384 × sizeof(clusterSlotStat) (hundreds of KB). It's no longer counted in `used_memory`, `INFO memory`, or maxmemory accounting.
- `zmalloc` calls the OOM handler and aborts on failure. `malloc` can return NULL, and `resetClusterStats()` would then `memset` a NULL pointer.
- It's inconsistent with the rest of the cluster code, which uses `zmalloc`/`zcalloc` everywhere (for example `server.cluster = zmalloc(...)` in `clusterInit`).

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="2138-2138"}
    server.cluster_slot_stats = zmalloc(CLUSTER_SLOTS*sizeof(clusterSlotStat));

Before this PR, `asmCron()` was the last call inside `clusterCron()`, so it saw the cluster state as just updated by that tick (node failure detection, `clusterUpdateState`, and so on). Now it runs first:

::diff{file="src/server.c" lines="1656-1660"}

In practice this probably just shifts ASM's reaction to topology changes by one 100ms tick. But it's an ordering change in a refactor that says it changes no behavior, and `beforeSleep` kept the original order (`clusterBeforeSleep` then `asmBeforeSleep`). Keeping `clusterCron(); asmCron();` would make both hooks consistent and keep the old semantics.

::suggestion{file="src/server.c" lines="1658-1659"}
            clusterCron();
            asmCron();
